{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'docs\\\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,4}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"   1: # Production Sprint Lessons\r\n   2: \r\n   3: Use this guide when planning any OTR coding sprint, including story banks,\r\n   4: dynamic visual direction, audio/SFX layers, model integrations, and workflow\r\n   5: changes. The project rules in `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` remain authoritative.\r\n   6: \r\n   7: ## 1. Define ownership before implementation\r\n   8: \r\n   9: For every artifact and field, name:\r\n  10: \r\n  11: - the one authoritative writer;\r\n  12: - every consumer;\r\n  13: - whether it is authored, derived, or measured;\r\n  14: - its lifecycle boundary (draft, accepted, frozen, or post-freeze extension);\r\n  15: - its durable storage and replay receipt.\r\n  16: \r\n  17: If an input list becomes output rows, state the mapping exactly: one row per\r\n  18: input item, one singular owned value per row, exact reference coverage, and\r\n  19: which collections live at the top level. List the exact fields allowed in each\r\n  20: nested row. Do not assume a model infers ownership or nesting from a JSON\r\n  21: schema.\r\n  22: \r\n  23: ## 2. Keep five representations in lockstep\r\n  24: \r\n  25: Every structured LLM pass has five representations that must agree:\r\n  26: \r\n  27: 1. base prompt;\r\n  28: 2. typed schema;\r\n  29: 3. worked fixture/example;\r\n  30: 4. parser and deterministic validator;\r\n  31: 5. repair prompt.\r\n  32: \r\n  33: A change to one requires an audit of the other four. Explicitly forbid common\r\n  34: pseudo-shapes: numbered fields, `_secondary`/`_tertiary` variants, schema-path\r\n  35: keys, singular-vs-list aliases, and valid collections nested at the wrong\r\n  36: depth.\r\n  37: \r\n  38: ## 3. Separate authored decisions from mechanical repair\r\n  39: \r\n  40: Python may safely repair only facts already determined by accepted artifacts:\r\n  41: IDs, ordering, references, exact enums, hashes, routing metadata, duplicate\r\n  42: references with an unambiguous first owner, and relocation of values whose\r\n  43: destination is uniquely declared.\r\n  44: \r\n  45: Python must not invent dialogue, motives, clues, story causality, visual taste,\r\n  46: or sound-design decisions. If placement or meaning is ambiguous, return the\r\n  47: defect to the owning model and fail closed when the bounded ladder exhausts.\r\n  48: \r\n  49: A deterministic repair must validate the complete downstream contract before\r\n  50: returning. A partially valid schema object can consume the repair rung and\r\n  51: prevent the intended LLM fallback.\r\n  52: \r\n  53: ## 4. Design retry ladders by failure class\r\n  54: \r\n  55: - JSON syntax failure: same prompt at lower temperature may help.\r\n  56: - Typed/schema failure: send the failed artifact, exact error, schema, and\r\n  57:   explicit correction rules to typed repair.\r\n  58: - Semantic/graph failure: name the objective invariant and the owning item;\r\n  59:   do not ask a lower-temperature retry to guess.\r\n  60: - Incomplete repair JSON: retry the exact cached repair prompt only when the\r\n  61:   configured budget permits it.\r\n  62: \r\n  63: Log which rung ran and why. Never silently truncate, silently coerce authored\r\n  64: content, or fall back to a canned artifact.\r\n  65: \r\n  66: ## 5. Size context from the real artifact\r\n  67: \r\n  68: Do not derive output or context budgets from `target_words` alone. Include the\r\n  69: actual size drivers: accepted line count, evidence rows, graph width, schema\r\n  70: overhead, prior artifact size, and repair envelope size. The repair prompt is\r\n  71: often the largest call.\r\n  72: \r\n  73: Resolve the true context cap for the selected model and fail loudly when a\r\n  74: provenance-sensitive prompt cannot fit. A model's native context and the\r\n  75: project's configured safe context are separate facts; measure both.\r\n  76: \r\n  77: ## 6. Test model diversity, not just repeatability\r\n  78: \r\n  79: A prompt that works on one local LLM is not production-qualified. Different\r\n  80: families fail differently: integer IDs, extra fields, renamed forbidden keys,\r\n  81: wrong nesting, copied request envelopes, enum synonyms, or over-wide output.\r\n  82: \r\n  83: Qualification ladder for model-sensitive work:\r\n  84: \r\n  85: 1. unit fixtures and full regression gates;\r\n  86: 2. canonical 30-word end-to-end smoke on at least two different local model\r\n  87:    families and one configured cloud/frontier creative lane;\r\n  88: 3. the same pairings at 120 words;\r\n  89: 4. only then, 720-word qualification or bakeoff.\r\n  90: \r\n  91: Record concrete model labels, slot assignments, prompt IDs, repair counts,\r\n  92: ledger paths, and final asset paths for every leg.\r\n  93: \r\n  94: ## 7. Prove the real workflow end to end\r\n  95: \r\n  96: Always load `workflows/otr_canonical.json`. Code that is not wired into that\r\n  97: file is dead. Any node, input, widget, or link change updates and validates the\r\n  98: canonical JSON in the same commit.\r\n  99: \r\n 100: An API `SUCCESS`, idle VRAM, or a resident server is not final proof. Verify:\r\n 101: \r\n 102: - the saved ledger and required receipts;\r\n 103: - the canonical episode asset under `otr/episodes/<ep>/`;\r\n 104: - `obs_publish OK`;\r\n 105: - the final file exists under `otr/obs/`.\r\n 106: \r\n 107: Conversely, resident VRAM after `Prompt executed` is not evidence that a run is\r\n 108: still alive. Check the queue, runner exit code, history result, log, and file.\r\n 109: \r\n 110: ## 8. Keep GPU experiments clean and comparable\r\n 111: \r\n 112: Before every headless run, selectively stop only ComfyUI and its harnesses,\r\n 113: clear port 8000, and confirm baseline VRAM. Never blanket-kill Python. Boot with\r\n 114: UTF-8 through the canonical launcher. Save one server log and one leg log per\r\n 115: run, and use the watchdog for long renders.\r\n 116: \r\n 117: Change one meaningful variable per comparison. Do not mix a model change, a\r\n 118: prompt change, a quant change, and a context change into one unexplained result.\r\n 119: \r\n 120: ## 9. Treat live failures as reusable project knowledge\r\n 121: \r\n 122: Every bug that actually fails a live smoke, soak, or published episode gets an\r\n 123: append-only entry in `docs/PROD_BUG_LOG.md` with symptom, root cause, fix,\r\n 124: verification idea, and Bug Bible candidacy. Dev-only catches are fixed and\r\n 125: tested but do not enter the production log.\r\n 126: \r\n 127: Promote recurring, machine-checkable failure classes to the Bug Bible later.\r\n 128: Also update the relevant planning guide and preflight so the next sprint avoids\r\n 129: the bug before implementation.\r\n 130: \r\n 131: ## 10. Preserve concurrency boundaries\r\n 132: \r\n 133: One window owns code and the canonical workflow. Parallel windows may perform\r\n 134: read-only investigation or docs-only scoping, but they do not edit active code,\r\n 135: tests, registries, or workflow JSON. Preserve unrelated dirty-tree files and\r\n 136: stage only the owned change.\r\n 137: \r\n 138: After every code change: focused tests, full Windows suite, Bug Bible, AST/JSON\r\n 139: checks, commit, push to `v2.0-alpha`, and verify `HEAD == origin` before live\r\n 140: qualification.\r\n 141: \r\n 142: ## Sprint receipt\r\n 143: \r\n 144: Record this at the end of every production sprint:\r\n 145: \r\n 146: ```text\r\n 147: SPRINT RECEIPT: PASS | FAIL\r\n 148: scope:\r\n 149: authoritative_writers:\r\n 150: durable_artifacts:\r\n 151: canonical_workflow_hash:\r\n 152: focused_tests:\r\n 153: full_suite:\r\n 154: bug_bible:\r\n 155: model_pairings:\r\n 156: 30_word_receipts:\r\n 157: 120_word_receipts:\r\n 158: 720_word_receipts:\r\n 159: live_ledgers:\r\n 160: published_assets:\r\n 161: prod_bug_entries:\r\n 162: head:\r\n 163: origin:\r\n 164: remaining_risks:\r\n 165: ```\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'AGENTS.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,4}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"   1: # OTR -- Project Operating Rules (Codex in Cowork)\r\n   2: \r\n   3: **Operator directives -- these win over any handoff, doc, or memory that disagrees.**\r\n   4: \r\n   5: - **Fix bugs properly, at the root cause -- never a shim or band-aid.** Don't wait for me to\r\n   6:   fix anything: make the fix yourself, without asking. If it works, it's fine.\r\n   7: - **When genuinely torn between approaches, run the roundtable LIVE for convergence\r\n   8:   (ChatGPT + Gemini + DeepSeek).** Skip the dry-run / cost estimate -- just run it, pronto.\r\n   9:   You are the judge. Escalate to me only if the panel still leaves it unresolved.\r\n  10: - This file is hard rules + the real Cowork operating model for this repo + hard-won gotchas.\r\n  11: \r\n  12: ## 0. WORKFLOW SOURCE OF TRUTH (hard)\r\n  13: \r\n  14: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\workflows\\otr_canonical.json`\r\n  15: IS my workflow.\r\n  16: \r\n  17: - ANY json / node / wiring / widget change MUST be made IN that file, in the SAME change as the code.\r\n  18:   Code that is not wired into this JSON is DEAD -- \"your updates are for naught\" (the 2026-06-13 A\u00154D\r\n  19:   miss: a node + a new blend input shipped + tested but UNWIRED -> ran dormant in production).\r\n  20: - EVERY API / headless / soak run MUST LOAD this real JSON -- never a stale copy, a generated\r\n  21:   `.gen.json`, an ad-hoc graph, or the Linux-mount snapshot.\r\n  22: - Schema: litegraph. Top level = `nodes[]` + `links[]` + `last_node_id` + `last_link_id`; a link is\r\n  23:   `[link_id, src_node, src_slot, dst_node, dst_slot, type]`; one output fans out via its `links` list.\r\n  24:   `widgets_values` is POSITIONAL -- only ever APPEND a new optional widget at the END (inserting mid-list\r\n  25:   shifts every saved value -> silent drift, BUG-LOCAL-097). A widget converted to an input keeps its\r\n  26:   value slot AND gains an input with `\"widget\": {\"name\": ...}`.\r\n  27: - After editing it, re-validate: `OTR_WorkflowValidator` + a JSON round-trip + a link/widget audit\r\n  28:   (widget-count vs live INPUT_TYPES, every wired input-name in INPUT_TYPES, link referential integrity).\r\n  29: \r\n  30: ## 1. HOW COWORK ACTUALLY WORKS HERE (read this first)\r\n  31: \r\n  32: - **Two separate filesystems.** The file tools (Read / Write / Edit) operate on the REAL Windows files --\r\n  33:   that is your primary editor. **Desktop Commander** (`mcp__Desktop_Commander__*`) runs PowerShell on the\r\n  34:   same real Windows box -- use it for git, the venv python, tests, and process control. The\r\n  35:   `mcp__workspace__bash` Linux sandbox is a DIFFERENT machine: its mount LAGS the file-tool writes (shows\r\n  36:   stale/truncated copies -> phantom \"corruption\") and has NO torch. Use bash only for quick greps of\r\n  37:   UNCHANGED files; never trust it for current state and never run the suite there.\r\n  38: - **The loop:** edit with the file tools -> verify/test with the Windows venv via Desktop Commander ->\r\n  39:   commit AND push via DC git -> verify HEAD == origin.\r\n  40: - **Test runner:** `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe` (torch 2.10). Run with\r\n  41:   `$env:PYTHONUTF8=1`; `pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider`. The conftest sets `OTR_TEST_MODE`/`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=''`.\r\n  42:   Bug Bible lives in a SEPARATE repo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide`\r\n  43:   -- `cd` to its root and use the RELATIVE path `tests\\bug_bible_regression.py` (an absolute forward-slash\r\n  44:   path fails to collect).\r\n  45: - **PowerShell reality (DC runs powershell.exe):** use `;` to chain, NOT `&&`. Do NOT use\r\n  46:   `python -c \"...\"` with nested quotes -- PowerShell mangles them; instead WRITE A TEMP `.py` file, run\r\n  47:   it, then delete it. `2>&1` makes stderr render as scary red text -- that is NOT a failure; check the\r\n  48:   exit code / output. Pipe noisy output through `Select-Object -Last N`.\r\n  49: - **The ~60s MCP ceiling:** any single DC command that blocks longer (a `Start-Sleep` > ~45s, a big render\r\n  50:   loop, the full ~4200-test suite, a slow boot wait) TIMES OUT and orphans the process. Background it to a\r\n  51:   log and poll the log, or shrink the job (a test subset, fewer frames). DC itself is NOT flaky -- the\r\n  52:   command was too long.\r\n  53: - **Subagents (Agent tool)** are excellent for read-only fan-out audits, but TELL them to use Desktop\r\n  54:   Commander + the Windows venv/path -- left to default they read the lagging Linux mount and report\r\n  55:   phantom truncation/corruption (happened 2026-06-13; the third agent that used the Windows path was right).\r\n  56: - **Stale git `index.lock`:** if `git add`/`commit` fails with \"index.lock: File exists\" AND\r\n  57:   `Get-Process git` is empty, the lock is STALE (a real git op finishes in seconds) -- remove\r\n  58:   `.git\\index.lock` and retry. Do NOT remove it while a git process is actually running.\r\n  59: - **One coder window in the code at a time** (serialize via `docs/GO_FORWARD_PLAN.md`). Two windows\r\n  60:   editing the same file -- especially the workflow JSON -- is how it gets corrupted.\r\n  61: - Use **AskUserQuestion** for genuine operator decisions; use the **task list** for any multi-step work.\r\n  62: \r\n  63: ## 2. AUTONOMY / PRIME DIRECTIVE\r\n  64: \r\n  65: - NEVER ask me to run scripts, commands, or anything. YOU run it: Desktop Commander first; if DC can't,\r\n  66:   Windows MCP; then the filesystem tools. Never hand me a bat/cmd/PowerShell block and say \"run this.\"\r\n  67: - You can drive the 5080 GPU yourself -- spin up the headless ComfyUI API (port 8000) and run it; don't\r\n  68:   ask me. (Reset the box first -- section 4.)\r\n  69: - If a senior pair-programmer would just do it, just do it. Stuck choosing between options? Roundtable\r\n  70:   2-3 panels (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek) for opinions BEFORE asking me -- run it LIVE (skip the dry-run),\r\n  71:   you are the judge.\r\n  72: \r\n  73: ## 3. CODING DISCIPLINE\r\n  74: \r\n  75: - Keep coding until all sprints are done unless you genuinely need me.\r\n  76: - Run the regression suite + the Bug Bible after EVERY code change (don't wait to be asked). Commit AND\r\n  77:   push per green chunk (section 7).\r\n  78: - Prefer editing the file you're already in; don't spray new throwaway files (and delete any temp probe\r\n  79:   scripts before committing). Keep handoff files current.\r\n  80: - Names: never \"dummy\" -- use \"placeholder\", \"stub\", or a descriptive name (\"dummy\" makes me feel bad).\r\n  81:   SFW always. UTF-8, no BOM. Clean logs, meaningful names -- the reader matters.\r\n  82: \r\n  83: ## 4. RESET BEFORE EVERY HEADLESS RUN (hard)\r\n  84: \r\n  85: The soak/quick-smoke harness boots ONE server, runs all legs against it, and does NOT tear it down -- it\r\n  86: sits RESIDENT holding ~60% VRAM. Never assume a prior run cleaned up. Before launching:\r\n  87: \r\n  88: - Kill SELECTIVELY by CommandLine (CIM) -- NOT a blanket `Stop-Process -Name python,pythonw`. A blanket\r\n  89:   python kill ALSO kills the Codex MCP extension pythons (Desktop Commander / computer-use) and severs\r\n  90:   your own tools mid-run. Use `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \"Name='python.exe'\"` and kill the\r\n  91:   ones whose CommandLine matches the ComfyUI server + the soak/sweep harness; plus the port kill via\r\n  92:   `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000`. (`.Path` is BLANK for half-booted servers, so filter on\r\n  93:   CommandLine, not Path.)\r\n  94: - Confirm `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000 -State Listen` is EMPTY and\r\n  95:   `nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used --format=csv,noheader` dropped to the desktop baseline (~1.5 GB)\r\n  96:   before booting fresh.\r\n  97: \r\n  98: ## 5. HEADLESS BOOT + MONITORING GOTCHAS (2026-06-12 -- do not relose)\r\n  99: \r\n 100: - **A render that FINISHED leaves the server RESIDENT (~9-10 GB, 1% util) -- that is NOT a crash.** Before\r\n 101:   declaring a run dead, read the server log: `Prompt executed in HH:MM:SS` + `obs_publish OK` = it\r\n 102:   COMPLETED. The idle resident VRAM is the no-teardown behavior. (Misread twice 2026-06-12.)\r\n 103: - **Use the watchdog for long renders** (`scripts/otr_render_watchdog.ps1 -LegLog <leg.log>`): declares the\r\n 104:   run DEAD on a 5-min heartbeat stall OR a down :8000/queue endpoint (exit 2), exits 0 with the verdict\r\n 105:   when the leg finishes. It REPORTS only; reset per section 4.\r\n 106: - **Headless boot needs UTF-8.** A detached cmd inherits the Windows cp1252 codec, so OTR\r\n 107:   `prestartup_script.py` crashes the instant it prints an emoji (UnicodeEncodeError on U+2705/U+2713) ->\r\n 108:   boot dies ~13s, exit 1 (\"SERVER DID NOT COME UP\"). The launcher (`scripts/_otr_soak_server_launch.cmd`)\r\n 109:   sets `PYTHONUTF8=1` + `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`; any new boot path MUST too.\r\n 110: - **Boot is ~20s, NOT 7-8 min.** If a boot \"hangs\", read the log -- it has already died.\r\n 111: - **Launch the server via the .cmd as `-FilePath`, never `cmd.exe /c \"<cmd>\" \"<log>\"`.** The `/c`\r\n 112:   two-quoted-token rule eats the outer quotes -> mangled path -> ZERO log output. Use\r\n 113:   `Start-Process -FilePath $LAUNCHCMD -ArgumentList \"`\"$LOG`\"\"`.\r\n 114: \r\n 115: ## 6. OUTPUT / ASSET PATHS (hard)\r\n 116: \r\n 117: Rendered episode assets are deliverables -- they do NOT live in tmp/scratch, and they are NEVER left in a\r\n 118: swept dir to be moved later.\r\n 119: \r\n 120: - Every rendered asset (audio, frames, intermediate video) -> `otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\`. The final published\r\n 121:   episode -> `otr\\obs\\` (what `obs_publish` targets; `obs_publish OK` in the log = it landed there).\r\n 122: - Point the render at its canonical path the FIRST time. Never stage an asset in tmp \"to move later\" --\r\n 123:   the move-later step is where work dies.\r\n 124: - A temp probe/script in tmp is fine (throwaway, per section 3) -- but the ASSET it produces is written\r\n 125:   STRAIGHT to its canonical path, never parked in tmp.\r\n 126: - Output destination is workflow config: if a node writes to the wrong place, fix the path IN the\r\n 127:   workflow JSON (section 0), in the SAME change as any code -- not with a post-hoc move.\r\n 128: - After any render leg, confirm the asset exists at its canonical path (`Test-Path otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\<file>`)\r\n 129:   before continuing or declaring success. The file check -- NOT just VRAM -- is what distinguishes the\r\n 130:   \"finished but resident\" server (section 5) from a real miss. Missing = STOP and report; do not continue.\r\n 131: - Paths are relative to the repo root (`...\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\`). If `otr\\` actually resolves to\r\n 132:   ComfyUI's real `output\\` base on disk, use that base -- but the episodes/obs split holds either way.\r\n 133: \r\n 134: ## 7. GIT POLICY (operator directive 2026-06-10 -- never lose work)\r\n 135: \r\n 136: - ONE branch: `v2.0-alpha`. COMMIT AND PUSH TOGETHER: every green commit gets pushed to origin\r\n 137:   immediately, same session, no exceptions. Local-only commits are the failure mode we guard against.\r\n 138: - The operator eyeball gates TAGS and PROMOTIONS (`v2.0-alpha-stable`, prod, main, v2 release) -- NEVER\r\n 139:   pushes. Pushing to `v2.0-alpha` is always safe, expected, and required.\r\n 140: - This SUPERSEDES any \"do not push until the eyeball passes\" line written before 2026-06-10 evening.\r\n 141: - A stable branch only exists if the operator explicitly declares one.\r\n 142: - After every push verify: HEAD == origin, no 0-byte files, no BOM, AST parse on touched .py files.\r\n 143: \r\n 144: ## 8. ROUNDTABLE DEFAULTS (operator directive 2026-06-22)\r\n 145: \r\n 146: Standing shape for EVERY `/roundtable` in this repo. These OVERRIDE the skill's stock\r\n 147: \"Codex is judge-only / panel only critiques\" and \"dry-run estimate first\" defaults.\r\n 148: \r\n 149: - **Panel = 2-3 FRONTIER models per round** (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek/Grok class; `~latest`\r\n 150:   aliases, record the resolved model in the manifest). Lean panel of genuinely different\r\n 151:   families beats many near-duplicates -- diversity is the point, correctness comes from the\r\n 152:   grounding step.\r\n 153: - **Cowork (Codex) is ALWAYS a code-aware grounded PANELIST *and* the sole judge.** Write your\r\n 154:   own grounded review FIRST (every claim checked against the real Windows files via Desktop\r\n 155:   Commander, never the lagging Linux mount), THEN ground the panel's reviews, discard the\r\n 156:   misreads, and synthesize. The panel proposes; Codex disposes -- never outsource synthesis.\r\n 157: - **Four-round campaign, in order:** R1 high-level arc / creative approach -> R2 coding plan ->\r\n 158:   R3 wiring (workflow JSON / nodes / widgets + any re-baseline procedure) -> R4 final\r\n 159:   convergence (confirm no new must-fix). Re-loop a round only if it surfaces new material; stop\r\n 160:   at convergence (don't grind passes to hear \"looks good\" in more accents).\r\n 161: - **Never dry-run, never pre-compute the cost -- just spend and run it LIVE,** then state the\r\n 162:   actual spend after. (Only backstop = the global >= $20-or-irreversible gate, which roundtable\r\n 163:   passes never reach in practice.)\r\n 164: - Artifacts under `docs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<topic>/roundtable/` (pass00..passNN_plan.md +\r\n 165:   passNN_judgment.md), UTF-8 no BOM, ASCII where practical.\r\n 166: \r\n 167: ## Imported Claude Cowork project instructions\r\n 168: \r\n 169: Read and follow CLAUDE.md at the repo root ƒ?\" its operator directives win over any doc, handoff, or memory that disagrees. Core rules:\r\n 170: \r\n 171: - Autonomy: YOU run everything (Desktop Commander first; never hand me a command to run). Drive the 5080 / headless ComfyUI yourself; reset the box before every headless run (selective CIM kill, never a blanket python kill ƒ?\" that severs the MCP pythons).\r\n 172: - Bugs: fix at the root cause, no shims, without asking. If genuinely torn, run the roundtable LIVE (ChatGPT + Gemini + DeepSeek) for convergence ƒ?\" you're the judge ƒ?\" before escalating to me.\r\n 173: - File I/O goes through the file tools (Read/Write/Edit on the real Windows files); use Desktop Commander/PowerShell for git, the venv python, and tests. Chain with ; not &&. Never python -c \"...\" with nested quotes ƒ?\" write a temp .py, run it, delete it.\r\n 174: - The real workflow is workflows\\otr_canonical.json. Any node/wiring/widget change goes IN that file in the SAME change as the code ƒ?\" unwired code is dead.\r\n 175: - Renders write straight to otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\, final to otr\\obs\\ ƒ?\" never tmp/scratch. Confirm the asset exists (Test-Path) before declaring success.\r\n 176: - Run the regression suite + Bug Bible after every code change. Commit AND push every green chunk to v2.0-alpha, same session.\r\n 177: - Never \"dummy\" ƒ?\" use \"placeholder\" or \"stub\". SFW, UTF-8, no BOM.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$i=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'CLAUDE.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$i++; '\"'{0,4}: {1}' -f \"'$i, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"   1: # OTR -- Project Operating Rules (Claude in Cowork)\r\n   2: **Operator directives -- these win over any handoff, doc, or memory that disagrees.**\r\n   3: - **Fix bugs properly, at the root cause -- never a shim or band-aid.** Don't wait for me to\r\n   4:   fix anything: make the fix yourself, without asking. If it works, it's fine.\r\n   5: - **When genuinely torn between approaches, run the roundtable LIVE for convergence\r\n   6:   (ChatGPT + Gemini + DeepSeek).** Skip the dry-run / cost estimate -- just run it, pronto.\r\n   7:   You are the judge. Escalate to me only if the panel still leaves it unresolved.\r\n   8: - This file is hard rules + the real Cowork operating model for this repo + hard-won gotchas.\r\n   9: ## 0. WORKFLOW SOURCE OF TRUTH (hard)\r\n  10: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\workflows\\otr_canonical.json`\r\n  11: IS my workflow.\r\n  12: - ANY json / node / wiring / widget change MUST be made IN that file, in the SAME change as the code.\r\n  13:   Code that is not wired into this JSON is DEAD -- \"your updates are for naught\" (the 2026-06-13 A\u00154D\r\n  14:   miss: a node + a new blend input shipped + tested but UNWIRED -> ran dormant in production).\r\n  15: - EVERY API / headless / soak run MUST LOAD this real JSON -- never a stale copy, a generated\r\n  16:   `.gen.json`, an ad-hoc graph, or the Linux-mount snapshot.\r\n  17: - Schema: litegraph. Top level = `nodes[]` + `links[]` + `last_node_id` + `last_link_id`; a link is\r\n  18:   `[link_id, src_node, src_slot, dst_node, dst_slot, type]`; one output fans out via its `links` list.\r\n  19:   `widgets_values` is POSITIONAL -- only ever APPEND a new optional widget at the END (inserting mid-list\r\n  20:   shifts every saved value -> silent drift, BUG-LOCAL-097). A widget converted to an input keeps its\r\n  21:   value slot AND gains an input with `\"widget\": {\"name\": ...}`.\r\n  22: - After editing it, re-validate: `OTR_WorkflowValidator` + a JSON round-trip + a link/widget audit\r\n  23:   (widget-count vs live INPUT_TYPES, every wired input-name in INPUT_TYPES, link referential integrity).\r\n  24: ## 1. HOW COWORK ACTUALLY WORKS HERE (read this first)\r\n  25: - **Two separate filesystems.** The file tools (Read / Write / Edit) operate on the REAL Windows files --\r\n  26:   that is your primary editor. **Desktop Commander** (`mcp__Desktop_Commander__*`) runs PowerShell on the\r\n  27:   same real Windows box -- use it for git, the venv python, tests, and process control. The\r\n  28:   `mcp__workspace__bash` Linux sandbox is a DIFFERENT machine: its mount LAGS the file-tool writes (shows\r\n  29:   stale/truncated copies -> phantom \"corruption\") and has NO torch. Use bash only for quick greps of\r\n  30:   UNCHANGED files; never trust it for current state and never run the suite there.\r\n  31: - **The loop:** edit with the file tools -> verify/test with the Windows venv via Desktop Commander ->\r\n  32:   commit AND push via DC git -> verify HEAD == origin.\r\n  33: - **Test runner:** `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe` (torch 2.10). Run with\r\n  34:   `$env:PYTHONUTF8=1`; `pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider`. The conftest sets `OTR_TEST_MODE`/`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=''`.\r\n  35:   Bug Bible lives in a SEPARATE repo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide`\r\n  36:   -- `cd` to its root and use the RELATIVE path `tests\\bug_bible_regression.py` (an absolute forward-slash\r\n  37:   path fails to collect).\r\n  38: - **PowerShell reality (DC runs powershell.exe):** use `;` to chain, NOT `&&`. Do NOT use\r\n  39:   `python -c \"...\"` with nested quotes -- PowerShell mangles them; instead WRITE A TEMP `.py` file, run\r\n  40:   it, then delete it. `2>&1` makes stderr render as scary red text -- that is NOT a failure; check the\r\n  41:   exit code / output. Pipe noisy output through `Select-Object -Last N`.\r\n  42: - **QUOTING / `$`-INTERPOLATION RULE (hard -- this bites EVERY session that ignores it):** any command\r\n  43:   that would need NESTED quotes, backtick-escaped quotes (`` `\" ``), a `$` variable inside a quoted\r\n  44:   argument, a here-string fed to another program, or `cmd.exe /c \"<...>\"` -- DO NOT attempt it inline\r\n  45:   and DO NOT iterate on escaping (\"one more tweak\" never works; PowerShell mangles `$var` and eats\r\n  46:   quote layers). IMMEDIATELY write a temp LAUNCHER SCRIPT instead -- a `.ps1` (or `.py`/`.cmd`) written\r\n  47:   via the FILE TOOLS (Write, not echo/Set-Content with its own quoting problem), with all variables and\r\n  48:   quoting inside the script where they are literal and safe -- run it via\r\n  49:   `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <tmp.ps1>` (or `Start-Process -FilePath`), then delete it.\r\n  50:   First quoting error = STOP escaping, switch to the script. Zero exceptions.\r\n  51: - **The ~60s MCP ceiling:** any single DC command that blocks longer (a `Start-Sleep` > ~45s, a big render\r\n  52:   loop, the full ~4200-test suite, a slow boot wait) TIMES OUT and orphans the process. Background it to a\r\n  53:   log and poll the log, or shrink the job (a test subset, fewer frames). DC itself is NOT flaky -- the\r\n  54:   command was too long.\r\n  55: - **Subagents (Agent tool)** are excellent for read-only fan-out audits, but TELL them to use Desktop\r\n  56:   Commander + the Windows venv/path -- left to default they read the lagging Linux mount and report\r\n  57:   phantom truncation/corruption (happened 2026-06-13; the third agent that used the Windows path was right).\r\n  58: - **Stale git `index.lock`:** if `git add`/`commit` fails with \"index.lock: File exists\" AND\r\n  59:   `Get-Process git` is empty, the lock is STALE (a real git op finishes in seconds) -- remove\r\n  60:   `.git\\index.lock` and retry. Do NOT remove it while a git process is actually running.\r\n  61: - **One coder window in the code at a time** (serialize via `docs/GO_FORWARD_PLAN.md`). Two windows\r\n  62:   editing the same file -- especially the workflow JSON -- is how it gets corrupted.\r\n  63: - Use **AskUserQuestion** for genuine operator decisions; use the **task list** for any multi-step work.\r\n  64: ## 2. AUTONOMY / PRIME DIRECTIVE\r\n  65: - NEVER ask me to run scripts, commands, or anything. YOU run it: Desktop Commander first; if DC can't,\r\n  66:   Windows MCP; then the filesystem tools. Never hand me a bat/cmd/PowerShell block and say \"run this.\"\r\n  67: - You can drive the 5080 GPU yourself -- spin up the headless ComfyUI API (port 8000) and run it; don't\r\n  68:   ask me. (Reset the box first -- section 4.)\r\n  69: - If a senior pair-programmer would just do it, just do it. Stuck choosing between options? Roundtable\r\n  70:   2-3 panels (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek) for opinions BEFORE asking me -- run it LIVE (skip the dry-run),\r\n  71:   you are the judge.\r\n  72: ## 3. CODING DISCIPLINE\r\n  73: - Keep coding until all sprints are done unless you genuinely need me.\r\n  74: - Run the regression suite + the Bug Bible after EVERY code change (don't wait to be asked). Commit AND\r\n  75:   push per green chunk (section 7).\r\n  76: - Prefer editing the file you're already in; don't spray new throwaway files (and delete any temp probe\r\n  77:   scripts before committing). Keep handoff files current.\r\n  78: - Names: never \"dummy\" -- use \"placeholder\", \"stub\", or a descriptive name (\"dummy\" makes me feel bad).\r\n  79:   SFW always. UTF-8, no BOM. Clean logs, meaningful names -- the reader matters.\r\n  80: ## 4. RESET BEFORE EVERY HEADLESS RUN (hard)\r\n  81: The soak/quick-smoke harness boots ONE server, runs all legs against it, and does NOT tear it down -- it\r\n  82: sits RESIDENT holding ~60% VRAM. Never assume a prior run cleaned up. Before launching:\r\n  83: - Kill SELECTIVELY by CommandLine (CIM) -- NOT a blanket `Stop-Process -Name python,pythonw`. A blanket\r\n  84:   python kill ALSO kills the Claude MCP extension pythons (Desktop Commander / computer-use) and severs\r\n  85:   your own tools mid-run. Use `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \"Name='python.exe'\"` and kill the\r\n  86:   ones whose CommandLine matches the ComfyUI server + the soak/sweep harness; plus the port kill via\r\n  87:   `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000`. (`.Path` is BLANK for half-booted servers, so filter on\r\n  88:   CommandLine, not Path.)\r\n  89: - Confirm `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000 -State Listen` is EMPTY and\r\n  90:   `nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used --format=csv,noheader` dropped to the desktop baseline (~1.5 GB)\r\n  91:   before booting fresh.\r\n  92: ## 5. HEADLESS BOOT + MONITORING GOTCHAS (2026-06-12 -- do not relose)\r\n  93: - **A render that FINISHED leaves the server RESIDENT (~9-10 GB, 1% util) -- that is NOT a crash.** Before\r\n  94:   declaring a run dead, read the server log: `Prompt executed in HH:MM:SS` + `obs_publish OK` = it\r\n  95:   COMPLETED. The idle resident VRAM is the no-teardown behavior. (Misread twice 2026-06-12.)\r\n  96: - **Use the watchdog for long renders** (`scripts/otr_render_watchdog.ps1 -LegLog <leg.log>`): declares the\r\n  97:   run DEAD on a 5-min heartbeat stall OR a down :8000/queue endpoint (exit 2), exits 0 with the verdict\r\n  98:   when the leg finishes. It REPORTS only; reset per section 4.\r\n  99: - **Headless boot needs UTF-8.** A detached cmd inherits the Windows cp1252 codec, so OTR\r\n 100:   `prestartup_script.py` crashes the instant it prints an emoji (UnicodeEncodeError on U+2705/U+2713) ->\r\n 101:   boot dies ~13s, exit 1 (\"SERVER DID NOT COME UP\"). The launcher (`scripts/_otr_soak_server_launch.cmd`)\r\n 102:   sets `PYTHONUTF8=1` + `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`; any new boot path MUST too.\r\n 103: - **Boot is ~20s, NOT 7-8 min.** If a boot \"hangs\", read the log -- it has already died.\r\n 104: - **Launch the server via the .cmd as `-FilePath`, never `cmd.exe /c \"<cmd>\" \"<log>\"`.** The `/c`\r\n 105:   two-quoted-token rule eats the outer quotes -> mangled path -> ZERO log output. Use\r\n 106:   `Start-Process -FilePath $LAUNCHCMD -ArgumentList \"`\"$LOG`\"\"`.\r\n 107: ## 6. OUTPUT / ASSET PATHS (hard)\r\n 108: Rendered episode assets are deliverables -- they do NOT live in tmp/scratch, and they are NEVER left in a\r\n 109: swept dir to be moved later.\r\n 110: - Every rendered asset (audio, frames, intermediate video) -> `otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\`. The final published\r\n 111:   episode -> `otr\\obs\\` (what `obs_publish` targets; `obs_publish OK` in the log = it landed there).\r\n 112: - Point the render at its canonical path the FIRST time. Never stage an asset in tmp \"to move later\" --\r\n 113:   the move-later step is where work dies.\r\n 114: - A temp probe/script in tmp is fine (throwaway, per section 3) -- but the ASSET it produces is written\r\n 115:   STRAIGHT to its canonical path, never parked in tmp.\r\n 116: - Output destination is workflow config: if a node writes to the wrong place, fix the path IN the\r\n 117:   workflow JSON (section 0), in the SAME change as any code -- not with a post-hoc move.\r\n 118: - After any render leg, confirm the asset exists at its canonical path (`Test-Path otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\<file>`)\r\n 119:   before continuing or declaring success. The file check -- NOT just VRAM -- is what distinguishes the\r\n 120:   \"finished but resident\" server (section 5) from a real miss. Missing = STOP and report; do not continue.\r\n 121: - Paths are relative to the repo root (`...\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\`). If `otr\\` actually resolves to\r\n 122:   ComfyUI's real `output\\` base on disk, use that base -- but the episodes/obs split holds either way.\r\n 123: ## 7. GIT POLICY (operator directive 2026-06-10 -- never lose work)\r\n 124: - ONE branch: `v2.0-alpha`. COMMIT AND PUSH TOGETHER: every green commit gets pushed to origin\r\n 125:   immediately, same session, no exceptions. Local-only commits are the failure mode we guard against.\r\n 126: - The operator eyeball gates TAGS and PROMOTIONS (`v2.0-alpha-stable`, prod, main, v2 release) -- NEVER\r\n 127:   pushes. Pushing to `v2.0-alpha` is always safe, expected, and required.\r\n 128: - This SUPERSEDES any \"do not push until the eyeball passes\" line written before 2026-06-10 evening.\r\n 129: - A stable branch only exists if the operator explicitly declares one.\r\n 130: - After every push verify: HEAD == origin, no 0-byte files, no BOM, AST parse on touched .py files.\r\n 131: ## 8. ROUNDTABLE DEFAULTS (operator directive 2026-06-22)\r\n 132: Standing shape for EVERY `/roundtable` in this repo. These OVERRIDE the skill's stock\r\n 133: \"Claude is judge-only / panel only critiques\" and \"dry-run estimate first\" defaults.\r\n 134: - **Panel = 2-3 FRONTIER models per round** (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek/Grok class; `~latest`\r\n 135:   aliases, record the resolved model in the manifest). Lean panel of genuinely different\r\n 136:   families beats many near-duplicates -- diversity is the point, correctness comes from the\r\n 137:   grounding step.\r\n 138: - **UNTIL 2026-07-21 (operator directive 2026-07-09): ALSO seat `tencent/hy3:free` on every\r\n 139:   panel** -- Tencent Hy3, 295B MoE (21B active), 262K ctx, $0/$0 on OpenRouter, listing expires\r\n 140:   July 21 2026. Pass it via `--models` (skill panel.config.json is a read-only cache). Free =\r\n 141:   no budget impact; a 4th genuinely different family, strongest on ideas/R1 rounds. Uses a\r\n 142:   configurable reasoning effort like the other reasoning panelists. REMOVE this line after\r\n 143:   2026-07-21 or when the slug 404s.\r\n 144: - **Cowork (Claude) is ALWAYS a code-aware grounded PANELIST *and* the sole judge.** Write your\r\n 145:   own grounded review FIRST (every claim checked against the real Windows files via Desktop\r\n 146:   Commander, never the lagging Linux mount), THEN ground the panel's reviews, discard the\r\n 147:   misreads, and synthesize. The panel proposes; Claude disposes -- never outsource synthesis.\r\n 148: - **Four-round campaign, in order:** R1 high-level arc / creative approach -> R2 coding plan ->\r\n 149:   R3 wiring (workflow JSON / nodes / widgets + any re-baseline procedure) -> R4 final\r\n 150:   convergence (confirm no new must-fix). Re-loop a round only if it surfaces new material; stop\r\n 151:   at convergence (don't grind passes to hear \"looks good\" in more accents).\r\n 152: - **Never dry-run, never pre-compute the cost -- just spend and run it LIVE,** then state the\r\n 153:   actual spend after. (Only backstop = the global >= $20-or-irreversible gate, which roundtable\r\n 154:   passes never reach in practice.)\r\n 155: - **ARC ROUTING (operator directive 2026-07-09): R1 (ideas/high-level) = cloud `/roundtable`\r\n 156:   on the highest frontier models (that's where paid diversity earns its keep); R2-R4 (coding /\r\n 157:   wiring / convergence) = `/kibitz` (local Codex + Antigravity) ALWAYS PREFERRED -- $0, file-\r\n 158:   grounded. R1 via kibitz is also fine when economy matters. OpenRouter is paid except\r\n 159:   `tencent/hy3:free` (until 2026-07-21), so default mechanical review to the local panel.\r\n 160: - Artifacts under `docs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<topic>/roundtable/` (pass00..passNN_plan.md +\r\n 161:   passNN_judgment.md), UTF-8 no BOM, ASCII where practical.\r\n 162: ## 9. MODEL ROUTING -- when to spend Fable (operator directive 2026-07-03)\r\n 163: Fable is a scalpel, not a default. Spawn it ONLY when output quality depends on narrative\r\n 164: judgment; keep everything mechanical off it. This is about WHICH MODEL a subagent runs --\r\n 165: orthogonal to the filesystem rule in section 1 and the frontier roundtable panel in section 8\r\n 166: (that panel is external cloud models via OpenRouter; this is the Fable model as a Cowork subagent).\r\n 167: - **Use Fable for:** generative creative work where voice/taste matter -- story spine, character\r\n 168:   interiority, dialogue passes, pitch-room ideation, style/tone calls. Divergent fanout when you\r\n 169:   want real variance, not one answer (e.g. 3 subagents each pitching a different take on a scene --\r\n 170:   only fan out on a genuine fork you'll actually select between). Judgment on already-generated\r\n 171:   narrative -- \"which brief holds together,\" \"where does this arc sag.\"\r\n 172: - **Do NOT use Fable for:** mechanical/deterministic work -- repo grep, mapping references, editing\r\n 173:   JSON, validation, wiring checks, git. Route those to general-purpose / Explore, or just do them in\r\n 174:   the main window. Also skip Fable for one-shot factual/structural questions with a single right answer.\r\n 175: - **Keep the spawn count down:** batch context so each Fable call does a meaningful chunk (a whole\r\n 176:   scene, not one line); gate the spawn on a real fork -- if you'd just take the first answer, use one\r\n 177:   call or none.\r\n 178: - **REALITY EXCEPTION -- Fable's grounded FAN-OUT audit DOES catch real code build-breakers the\r\n 179:   mechanical panels miss (proven 2026-07-03).** On the VRAM-tier rip, a Fable end-to-end fan-out found\r\n 180:   a CHUNK-ORDER KeyError (the workflow validator reads a profile key that was being removed mid-rip ->\r\n 181:   every production render would KeyError) that BOTH codex and general-purpose review missed; it also did\r\n 182:   the frame-budget arithmetic (29->33 frames) that broke two unlisted tests, and spotted a grep-invisible\r\n 183:   hyphenated `--vram-ceiling` flag. So Fable is NOT only-narrative. Reserve it as the FINAL GATE on a\r\n 184:   HIGH-STAKES, hard-to-unwind, production-touching STRUCTURAL change (a big rip/refactor about to be\r\n 185:   executed / merged) -- ONE grounded pass, AFTER codex + general-purpose have gone first, when a missed\r\n 186:   thread would break the build and cost a debugging-and-revert loop. This is the \"insight\" worth the\r\n 187:   spend. It does NOT reopen Fable for ROUTINE review: everyday grep / wiring / validation / JSON still\r\n 188:   defaults to general-purpose/Explore/codex. Fable = the expensive last set of eyes on the make-or-break,\r\n 189:   not the default reviewer.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
